WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.649 --> 00:00:09.969
India is the World’s largest movie producer.

00:00:09.969 --> 00:00:11.420
We’ve all heard of Bollywood, right?

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:12.940
But let me ask you a question…

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:15.610
what’s the SECOND largest movie producer?

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:21.940
What comes after India?

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:23.619
…
The answer is NIGERIA.

00:00:23.619 --> 00:00:26.560
Yes, I’m sure almost nobody guessed that
right.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:31.430
But, my dear viewers, so-called NOLLYWOOD
produces many more titles a year than Hollywood

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:32.430
itself.

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:35.790
But, of course, nobody knows this because
Africa is the forgotten continent.

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:39.050
African politics and economics hardly appear
in the newspapers.

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:42.830
And this is why we can say that Africans are
witnessing a silent miracle.

00:00:42.830 --> 00:00:46.799
Until the 2000s, this region seemed completely
doomed.

00:00:46.799 --> 00:00:51.500
But since the beginning of the new century,
Africans have tripled their income.

00:00:51.500 --> 00:01:01.339
Countries like Ethiopia are growing at a 10%
growth rate year after year.

00:01:01.339 --> 00:01:03.309
And I know what you’re gonna tell me!

00:01:03.309 --> 00:01:04.309
Come on Simon!

00:01:04.309 --> 00:01:05.309
These are just statistics.

00:01:05.309 --> 00:01:08.130
Is life really changing for the average African?

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:14.170
Look… if you’re expecting to see skyscrapers
and caravans of BMWs on the streets of Nairobi,

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:16.740
then, yes, you’re going to be disappointed.

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:18.850
We’re not there, yet.

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:22.310
In fact, there are still regions of the continent
stuck in extreme poverty.

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:26.470
That would be the case in the People’s Republic
of Congo and the Central African Republic.

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:28.550
But other regions are improving.

00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:33.740
For example, Addis Ababa, the Ethiopian capital,
is starting to get some supermarkets!

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:36.490
This might seem like a very trivial thing
to you.

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:40.420
But it’s a giant step if you remember that
30 years ago, the whole country was going

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:42.720
through one of the worst famines in history.

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:45.570
But wait a minute because there is more!

00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:49.979
We are starting to see how African companies
are expanding internationally.

00:01:49.979 --> 00:01:59.710
For example, Ethiopian airlines or M-PESA,
a mobile phone payment platform.

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:00.909
So now you might wonder…

00:02:00.909 --> 00:02:02.670
Why is Africa growing like this?

00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:04.630
What has changed in the continent?

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:06.480
The answer is clear: PEACE.

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:12.010
You see, the first requirement for a country
to develop is for it not to have wars.

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:15.510
After that, we can discuss political systems,
tax policies or whatever.

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:19.980
But a society that lives on a battlefield
can hardly prosper.

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:23.480
But now things are starting to change… is
that true, Xander?

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:24.480
Absolutely, Simon!

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:30.079
For decades, Africa was a really violent continent:
wars for independence, tribal clashes and

00:02:30.079 --> 00:02:32.579
even genocide.

00:02:32.579 --> 00:02:43.709
Today, however, the conflicts are less frequent.

00:02:43.709 --> 00:02:49.139
In the year 1990, an average of 45 per 100.000
africans died in conflicts.

00:02:49.139 --> 00:02:52.249
Today, this figure has dropped to 8 per 100.000.

00:02:52.249 --> 00:02:57.519
And, of course, this has helped trade and
foreign investment, especially with China.

00:02:57.519 --> 00:03:01.069
Nevertheless, as we said before, there is
still a lot of work to be done.

00:03:01.069 --> 00:03:03.650
Africa is far from being a prosperous region.

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:05.519
They need to take the next step.

00:03:05.519 --> 00:03:07.230
And what is that?

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:09.569
Well, that would be industrialization.

00:03:09.569 --> 00:03:12.799
So far, Africa remains a rural continent.

00:03:12.799 --> 00:03:16.219
Except for a few exceptions, everything you
can buy in Africa is a raw material.

00:03:16.219 --> 00:03:20.549
And it’s hard to grow a modern economy based
on agriculture and mining.

00:03:20.549 --> 00:03:26.099
So, this the context in which this news takes
place.

00:03:26.099 --> 00:03:33.670
The Largest Free Trade Deal in Nearly a Quarter-Century
Seeks to Make Africa a Single Market

00:03:33.670 --> 00:03:34.670
Yes, you heard that right.

00:03:34.670 --> 00:03:38.959
The goal of this treaty is to make an African
version of the European Single Market, which

00:03:38.959 --> 00:03:42.579
was the seed for what we know today as the
European Union.

00:03:42.579 --> 00:03:46.099
Since May 30th 2019, this treaty has been
a reality.

00:03:46.099 --> 00:03:49.400
We’re talking about 52 countries who have
signed it.

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:55.769
In fact, only Benin, Nigeria and Eritrea have
refused to enter the so called AfCFTA or just

00:03:55.769 --> 00:03:56.769
CFTA.

00:03:56.769 --> 00:03:57.769
So now the question is…

00:03:57.769 --> 00:04:01.610
Can this treaty change things… or is it
just a bunch of nice words?

00:04:01.610 --> 00:04:04.739
Can a free trade agreement really improve
lives in Africa?

00:04:04.739 --> 00:04:09.599
And, why doesn’t Nigeria, the country with
the largest African economy, want to sign

00:04:09.599 --> 00:04:10.599
it?

00:04:10.599 --> 00:04:13.469
Today we are going to answer all of these
questions but, before we do, let’s take

00:04:13.469 --> 00:04:16.870
a look back at the history.

00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:22.240
NO MARKET, NO INDUSTRY
When we talk about Africa, we should remember

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:25.740
one thing: 55 countries and 1,3 billion people.

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:28.259
We shouldn’t really talk about Africa like
it’s a country.

00:04:28.259 --> 00:04:32.520
Then again, there is one thing that most of
the African countries have in common: their

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:33.780
colonial past.

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:36.889
Most of these nations have been independent
for less than 50 years.

00:04:36.889 --> 00:04:41.650
And, believe me, they still maintain their
bonds with their previous colonizers.

00:04:41.650 --> 00:04:44.630
In many ways, the idea of independence is
pretty much a delusion.

00:04:44.630 --> 00:04:49.400
Look, in a normal country, the main trading
partner is that country’s neighbours.

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:53.830
America trades with Mexico and Canada, France
trades with Germany, Germany trades with The

00:04:53.830 --> 00:04:57.800
Netherlands… but African countries are different
animals in this regard.

00:04:57.800 --> 00:04:58.800
For example…

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:01.310
Ivory Coast is neighbours Ghana and Liberia.

00:05:01.310 --> 00:05:04.310
But who is Ivory Coast’s main trading partner?

00:05:04.310 --> 00:05:05.310
No!

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:06.610
It’s not their neighbours.

00:05:06.610 --> 00:05:08.900
Their main Trading partner is The Netherlands
and…

00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:12.790
France which, surprise, surprise, it is its
former colonizer.

00:05:12.790 --> 00:05:19.680
And of course, this trend is changing but
not in the way you might expect.

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:23.530
If you’ve been following VisualPolitik for
a long time, you already know that China has

00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:26.650
been increasing its sway in Africa over the
past several years.

00:05:26.650 --> 00:05:28.990
And not only are the Asians playing a role
here!

00:05:28.990 --> 00:05:32.330
Just look at this news!

00:05:32.330 --> 00:05:39.120
China’s coming slowdown opens doors to expanding
African trade with India

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:40.120
But not only that!

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:44.860
In the last 15 years, trade with Africa has
tripled with other countries like Turkey and

00:05:44.860 --> 00:05:45.860
Indonesia.

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:49.449
Today, Asia represents 45% of the African
foreign trade.

00:05:49.449 --> 00:05:50.449
Europe is 30%.

00:05:50.449 --> 00:05:55.610
So, as you can see, there’s only a little
share for the inter african trade.

00:05:55.610 --> 00:05:57.699
And I know what you’re thinking now!

00:05:57.699 --> 00:05:59.259
So what?

00:05:59.259 --> 00:06:01.560
Don’t we live in a globalized economy?

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:05.070
What’s the problem with Trading with Asia
or Europe?

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:06.419
Well… it is a problem.

00:06:06.419 --> 00:06:09.530
Think about it… what does Europe or Asia
buy from Africa?

00:06:09.530 --> 00:06:15.460
Yes, you guessed it: raw materials like oil,
gas, gold, uranium… in other words, everything

00:06:15.460 --> 00:06:17.509
except manufactured goods.

00:06:17.509 --> 00:06:20.210
And there is a reason for this.

00:06:20.210 --> 00:06:25.800
Let’s look at an example.

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:28.600
Rwanda Motorcycle Company was born in 2017.

00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:31.190
They manufacture low cost motorbikes.

00:06:31.190 --> 00:06:32.490
Would you buy one of those?

00:06:32.490 --> 00:06:33.490
Probably not.

00:06:33.490 --> 00:06:36.080
Their bikes are no match for a Suzuki or a
Honda.

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:41.819
So no Asian or European country would go for
a Rwandan model when they could have a Japanese

00:06:41.819 --> 00:06:43.229
or American one.

00:06:43.229 --> 00:06:46.500
So… where could Rwandan motorcycles find
a market?

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:48.759
Of course, in other African countries!

00:06:48.759 --> 00:06:52.990
In fact, Africa is a great place for them
because there are almost no competitors.

00:06:52.990 --> 00:06:57.789
If you live in Congo, good luck finding a
BMW shop to buy a bike from.

00:06:57.789 --> 00:07:03.479
So in many countries, these Rwandan entrepreneurs
have a big blue ocean of market opportunities

00:07:03.479 --> 00:07:10.150
to exploit.

00:07:10.150 --> 00:07:14.580
This explains why, despite African countries
trading so little with each other, most of

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:17.400
the things they trade are manufactured goods.

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:22.139
In other words: if Africa wants to have industry,
they need to trade with each other more.

00:07:22.139 --> 00:07:24.189
So what’s the problem here?

00:07:24.189 --> 00:07:28.819
The problem is that things in Africa are never
easy and trade… is harder than anything.

00:07:28.819 --> 00:07:33.110
There are lots of tariffs and bureaucracy
that makes it almost impossible for local

00:07:33.110 --> 00:07:34.870
companies to cross borders.

00:07:34.870 --> 00:07:39.099
If you want to send your Rwandan motorbike
to neighbouring Congo, you’ll have to pay

00:07:39.099 --> 00:07:40.289
tariffs of up to 20%.

00:07:40.289 --> 00:07:43.490
But you’ll also have to bribe the guard
at Customs.

00:07:43.490 --> 00:07:48.479
Otherwise, your merch could be waiting there
for months.

00:07:48.479 --> 00:07:55.030
Add to this the insecurity, the gangs and
the warlords that are still present in many

00:07:55.030 --> 00:07:56.030
regions.

00:07:56.030 --> 00:08:00.419
Don’t forget that, just a few years ago,
many of these countries were killing each

00:08:00.419 --> 00:08:01.820
other’s citizens.

00:08:01.820 --> 00:08:07.009
So again, a bike manufacturer in Rwanda can
hardly survive with as small and poor a market

00:08:07.009 --> 00:08:08.009
as the local one.

00:08:08.009 --> 00:08:11.620
If they want to survive, they need to expand
to their neighbours.

00:08:11.620 --> 00:08:16.069
In other words, protectionism is making it
almost impossible for Africa to develop.

00:08:16.069 --> 00:08:21.490
But pay attention now, because this might
change.

00:08:21.490 --> 00:08:27.180
THE AFRICAN UNION
‘Blood Diamond’ is a Hollywood movie that

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:31.599
talks about how we can end up killing each
other just for some precious minerals.

00:08:31.599 --> 00:08:36.419
The conclusion of this movie is that the West
just wants Africa’s resources.

00:08:36.419 --> 00:08:37.479
Nothing more.

00:08:37.479 --> 00:08:41.310
Presented with this situation, many might
think that Africans can’t get themselves

00:08:41.310 --> 00:08:44.810
together and they need a good old Western
helping hand.

00:08:44.810 --> 00:08:46.580
But, of course, this is VisualPolitik.

00:08:46.580 --> 00:08:50.330
You know we think differently…

00:08:50.330 --> 00:08:56.580
“Africa’s story has been written by others;
we need to own our problems and solutions

00:08:56.580 --> 00:09:01.240
and write our story”—Paul Kagame
Paul Kagame is the Rwandan president.

00:09:01.240 --> 00:09:05.270
In this video we told you about his light
side and his dark side.

00:09:05.270 --> 00:09:09.560
His model country is Singapore so, you can
imagine that he’s not a big fan of democracy

00:09:09.560 --> 00:09:10.560
or human rights.

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:14.850
But, from an economic point of view, he has
become an African champion for prosperity.

00:09:14.850 --> 00:09:19.150
The thing is, apart from governing Rwanda,
Kagame is also the president of the African

00:09:19.150 --> 00:09:20.150
Union.

00:09:20.150 --> 00:09:23.890
This organization was created in 2001, inspired
by the European Union.

00:09:23.890 --> 00:09:27.950
Their big dream is to turn Africa into a single
market, just like Europe is.

00:09:27.950 --> 00:09:32.270
And, so far, it seems like they’re achieving
their goal.

00:09:32.270 --> 00:09:37.390
Africa's free trade area comes into force
as Gambia ratification

00:09:37.390 --> 00:09:42.770
Yes, my dear viewers: it’s the CFTA I mentioned
earlier in the video, the African Continental

00:09:42.770 --> 00:09:43.850
Free Trade Agreement.

00:09:43.850 --> 00:09:47.600
Their ultimate goal is to have a common currency
for the whole continent.

00:09:47.600 --> 00:09:49.820
Something similar to the Euro, but in Africa.

00:09:49.820 --> 00:09:55.080
We might have to wait 15 to 20 years to witness
that, but they are trying hard.

00:09:55.080 --> 00:10:03.530
But, OK, OK, hold on just a minute because
not everything is sunshine and rainbows.

00:10:03.530 --> 00:10:06.800
As we always say, the devil is in the details.

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:12.500
You see, in this world we have free trade
agreements and… “free trade agreements”.

00:10:12.500 --> 00:10:15.500
An example of the latter would be MERCOSUR.

00:10:15.500 --> 00:10:19.670
Mercosur was born in order to create a free
trade area in South America.

00:10:19.670 --> 00:10:20.670
Has it worked?

00:10:20.670 --> 00:10:22.160
The answer is no.

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:26.590
The countries who signed this document were
not willing to cede sovereignty.

00:10:26.590 --> 00:10:30.520
Nobody was willing to remove tariffs and let
their local companies compete with foreign

00:10:30.520 --> 00:10:31.520
ones.

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:33.790
The Mercosur treaty is full of exceptions.

00:10:33.790 --> 00:10:39.130
Yes, it has nice words in it but when you
look at the reality, you realize the trade

00:10:39.130 --> 00:10:41.810
between its members hasn’t increased really.

00:10:41.810 --> 00:10:45.070
Something similar could happen with this African
free trade area.

00:10:45.070 --> 00:10:49.550
For example, the main African economies: South
Africa and Nigeria, are not big fans of this.

00:10:49.550 --> 00:10:55.570
South Africa signed it at the last minute
but Nigeria… well… this is what Nigeria

00:10:55.570 --> 00:10:56.880
said.

00:10:56.880 --> 00:11:06.040
CFTA: I won’t allow Nigeria to be dumping
ground —Muhammadu Buhari, President of Nigeria

00:11:06.040 --> 00:11:10.100
Basically, many governments are reluctant
to lose control of the small industries they

00:11:10.100 --> 00:11:11.100
already have.

00:11:11.100 --> 00:11:13.870
Besides, their main income is what they collect
in tariffs.

00:11:13.870 --> 00:11:19.080
Don’t forget that, in a country like Nigeria,
there is no big and regulated job market so

00:11:19.080 --> 00:11:21.020
it’s hard to collect income tax.

00:11:21.020 --> 00:11:26.010
In most cases, African governments get their
money from the only thing they can control:

00:11:26.010 --> 00:11:27.010
foreign trade.

00:11:27.010 --> 00:11:28.010
But there is more to this!

00:11:28.010 --> 00:11:31.410
This CFTA treaty generates as many doubts
as hopes.

00:11:31.410 --> 00:11:35.440
You see, a treaty like this usually takes
decades to negotiate.

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:38.310
This one was done in just 4 years.

00:11:38.310 --> 00:11:42.320
And, in their desire to have as many countries
as possible, they’ve left lots of things

00:11:42.320 --> 00:11:45.080
off of the negotiating table.

00:11:45.080 --> 00:11:51.370
The final document is vague and generic.

00:11:51.370 --> 00:11:56.420
It doesn’t say anything clear about competition
clauses, investments or intellectual property

00:11:56.420 --> 00:11:57.420
rights.

00:11:57.420 --> 00:12:01.570
There is no list of what goods you can trade
freely and which ones will be protected.

00:12:01.570 --> 00:12:05.820
In theory, they have committed to removing
tariffs on 90% of the products over the next

00:12:05.820 --> 00:12:06.820
decade.

00:12:06.820 --> 00:12:10.700
But, of course… governments can push the
most important goods inside of that 10%.

00:12:10.700 --> 00:12:15.380
So, if you only liberalize trade on the goods
that you don’t actually sell… then you

00:12:15.380 --> 00:12:16.850
won’t have real free trade.

00:12:16.850 --> 00:12:20.520
But hold on a second because there are many
more problems!

00:12:20.520 --> 00:12:25.910
Of course, the CFTA would be wonderful as
a way to incentivize the emergence of new

00:12:25.910 --> 00:12:26.910
industries.

00:12:26.910 --> 00:12:29.740
But… so far, Africa relies heavily on agriculture.

00:12:29.740 --> 00:12:32.020
And what do African farmers think?

00:12:32.020 --> 00:12:38.250
Well… many of them are not exactly happy.

00:12:38.250 --> 00:12:42.330
Smugglers Thrive as Nigeria Tries to Keep
Foreign Rice Away

00:12:42.330 --> 00:12:45.770
n this case, Nigeria is afraid of neighbouring
Benin.

00:12:45.770 --> 00:12:48.390
Benin has become a big importer of Thai rice.

00:12:48.390 --> 00:12:50.700
Apparently, Thai rice is high quality and
affordable.

00:12:50.700 --> 00:12:55.410
If Nigeria suddenly opens trade with Benin,
its local rice producers might have a hard

00:12:55.410 --> 00:12:56.410
time.

00:12:56.410 --> 00:12:58.700
And this is where the big time dilemma comes
in.

00:12:58.700 --> 00:13:04.450
Should we risk the agriculture that we already
have in exchange for would-be brand new industries…

00:13:04.450 --> 00:13:12.190
or do we leave everything as it is?

00:13:12.190 --> 00:13:13.810
And this is the real debate.

00:13:13.810 --> 00:13:18.491
According to the United Nations, if the CFTA
turns out the way it was meant to, inter African

00:13:18.491 --> 00:13:21.630
trade could grow 52% in the year 2022.

00:13:21.630 --> 00:13:24.860
This is only 3 years after the year that we
made this video.

00:13:24.860 --> 00:13:29.240
But, of course, if the member countries keep
adding exceptions and mistrusting each other,

00:13:29.240 --> 00:13:31.650
we could end up with a Mercosur kind of thing.

00:13:31.650 --> 00:13:35.850
In other words, our friends from the Rwandan
Motorcycle Company would keep struggling to

00:13:35.850 --> 00:13:37.330
open markets elsewhere.

00:13:37.330 --> 00:13:43.280
And… remember, with no market there is no
industry at all.

00:13:43.280 --> 00:13:47.540
So the question now goes to you…

00:13:47.540 --> 00:13:51.860
Do you think the CFTA will go in a European
Union-like direction… or will it go with

00:13:51.860 --> 00:13:54.380
a more Mercosur-like style?

00:13:54.380 --> 00:13:55.910
Should Nigeria sign it?

00:13:55.910 --> 00:13:58.890
Will we witness the industrialization of the
African continent?

